<ArticleLayout slug="how-to-specify-chafing-dishes-for-a-hotel-buffet">
  <p>
    A chafing dish is the most-photographed and least-considered object in a hotel. It is bought on
    price, replaced on failure, and specified — when it is specified at all — by copying whatever the
    last property used. What follows is the specification a purchase manager is normally left to
    guess at.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with pan size, not with the dish</h2>
  <p>
    Everything else follows from the food pan. The industry standard is Gastronorm, and the two sizes
    that matter for a buffet are GN 1/1 (530 × 325 mm, roughly 9 litres at standard depth) and GN 2/3.
    Specify the pan and the dish is decided.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason this matters more than it sounds: if your chafing dish takes a proprietary pan rather
    than a standard GN, you are locked to one supplier for spares for the life of the unit. Ask the
    question directly — <strong>"does this take a standard GN 1/1 pan?"</strong> — and treat a vague
    answer as a no.
  </p>

  <h2>Lid action decides your service, not your looks</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Lid type</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Roll-top</strong></td>
        <td>Long buffet lines where staff and guests pass in one direction. Opens one-handed and stays open.</td>
        <td>The hinge. This is where a chafer dies. Ask whether the pin is machined or a folded tab.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Hydraulic / gas strut</strong></td>
        <td>Centrepiece islands. The lid stops wherever you leave it.</td>
        <td>A strut working at its rated limit will creep shut by year two. Specify one rated well above the lid weight.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Lift-off</strong></td>
        <td>Tight corners and back-of-house. Cheapest and simplest.</td>
        <td>The lid has to go somewhere. In practice it goes on the counter, which is why lift-off looks untidy at volume.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Glass lid</strong></td>
        <td>Breakfast and live counters, where the food is the display.</td>
        <td>Thermal shock. Never run cold water over a hot lid.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Fuel or induction</h2>
  <p>
    Gel fuel is cheap, portable and unpredictable. It burns at a rate you cannot control, it needs
    replacing mid-service, and a guest standing close can smell it. Induction gives you a temperature
    you set and hold, no combustion, and no consumable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical answer for most properties is both: specify chafers with an induction-compatible
    base plate <em>and</em> a fuel holder. You run induction where there is power and fuel where there
    is not — a garden wedding, a power cut, a satellite station across the lawn.
  </p>

  <h2>How many do you actually need?</h2>
  <p>These are working numbers, not a formula, and they assume a two-hour service:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One full-size chafer per 40–50 covers per hot dish.</strong> A 300-cover wedding with six hot dishes needs six to eight units on the line, not six.</li>
    <li><strong>One soup station per 100 covers</strong>, positioned at the head of the line where the queue forms.</li>
    <li><strong>Split-pan minis for anything that is a side or a condiment.</strong> Running eight small items in full-size pans wastes counter and wastes food.</li>
    <li><strong>Add 10% for rotation.</strong> Something is always being washed, repaired or re-finished.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The four questions to put to a supplier</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Which steel grade, and where?</strong> 304 for anything food touches. 201 is acceptable in a frame and is a fifth cheaper — but a supplier who will not tell you which is which is telling you something.</li>
    <li><strong>How is the hinge made?</strong> A machined stainless pin outlasts a folded tab by years. This single detail predicts the life of the unit better than anything else.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard GN pans?</strong> See above.</li>
    <li><strong>What is the spares position in three years?</strong> Fuel holders, hinge pins, handles and pan gaskets. If the answer is "buy a new one", price accordingly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Finish, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    Mirror polish looks best on day one and worst on day ninety, because every service mark sits on
    the surface. A hammered or satin face scatters light in the same way that scuffing does, so marks
    disappear into it. For a property doing daily volume, hammered is the cheaper decision even when
    it is the more expensive purchase.
  </p>
  <p>
    We go into this in more detail in <a href="/journal/brass-copper-or-stainless-steel-for-hospitality">the guide to choosing the metal</a>,
    and every chafer we make is listed with its full specification in
    <a href="/collections/chafing-dishes">the chafing dish collection</a>.
  </p>
